======================================================================================================================
ITEM 98 -- what the external field does to the Oort constants
======================================================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A -- A and B carry no framework content, and the data say so
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Ou+24 rotation curve, fitted linearly over R = 5-14 kpc: V(R0) = 231.3 km/s, dV/dR = -1.23 km/s/kpc
    A - B = V/R0     : rotation curve 28.28   Oort 27.20 +- 0.57 km/s/kpc
    A + B = -dV/dR   : rotation curve +1.23   Oort +3.40 +- 0.57 km/s/kpc
  [PASS] 98a A and B are a consistency check between two independent datasets and NOT a test of gravity: both reduce to the local circular speed and its slope, which any theory fitting the rotation curve reproduces by construction.  Recorded because item 98 implies otherwise   (A - B from the rotation curve is 28.28 against Oort's 27.20 +- 0.57 (1.9 sigma -- the known offset between tracer populations); A + B is +1.23 against +3.40.  Neither discriminates)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B -- the Sun's azimuth relative to the external field's in-plane direction
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  the total external field points to (l, b) = (268.5, +33.0); its in-plane direction is l = 268.5 and it makes gamma = 57.0 deg with the disc normal
  the Sun lies from the Galactic centre toward l = 180, so its azimuth from the field direction is psi_sun = 88.5 deg.  C and K scale as sin(m psi) and the shifts in A and B as cos(m psi), so the Sun sits essentially AT the maximum of the C, K response (sin = 0.9997) and at the null of the A, B response (cos = +0.0262)
  [PASS] 98b the geometry is maximally favourable, which is what makes this a real bound rather than a geometrical accident: the Sun sits within a couple of degrees of the azimuth at which the external field's non-axisymmetric term contributes ALL of its effect to C and K and none to A and B   (psi_sun = 88.5 deg, sin(psi_sun) = 0.9997)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C -- the streaming motions the external field forces, and the C and K they give
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       footing       e_N   v_R m=1  v_phi m=1   v_R m=2    C_pred    K_pred   (km/s and km/s/kpc)
     canonical   0.01273     0.053      0.189     0.001   -0.0138   +0.0039
           alt   0.01055     0.051      0.202     0.001   -0.0143   +0.0053
  measured (Bovy 2017): C = -3.2 +- 0.4, K = -3.3 +- 0.6 km/s/kpc, both conventionally attributed to the bar and the spiral arms
  [PASS] 98c the framework's non-axisymmetric term is REAL and is far below the measurement: the external field forces streaming motions of order a km/s at the Sun and contributes only a few per cent of the measured C.  So the Oort constants do not detect it -- and, equally, the framework is not in conflict with them   (|C_pred| = 0.0143 km/s/kpc at most, against a measured -3.2 +- 0.4: 0.4 per cent of the central value and 0.04 of its error bar)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART D -- turning it round: a purely local bound on the Milky Way's external field
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Because C_pred grows with e_N, the measured C and its error could in principle be inverted into an upper limit on the external field acting on the Galaxy -- from local stellar kinematics alone, with no reference to any galaxy survey.  The table below is what that inversion actually gives, and it is a good deal less useful than it sounds.
          e_N   C_pred (km/s/kpc)   |K_pred|  C_pred/e_N  linear?
       0.0050             -0.0055     0.0015     -1.0933      yes
       0.0079             -0.0086     0.0024     -1.0865      yes
       0.0126             -0.0136     0.0039     -1.0838      yes
       0.0199             -0.0216     0.0063     -1.0848      yes
       0.0316             -0.0344     0.0104     -1.0895      yes
       0.0501             -0.0550     0.0170     -1.0974       --
       0.0794             -0.0879     0.0280     -1.1068       --
       0.1259             -0.1401     0.0469     -1.1124       --
       0.1996             -0.2193     0.0815     -1.0987       --
       0.3165             -0.3233     0.1534     -1.0213       --
       0.5018             -0.3768     0.3418     -0.7508       --
       0.7956             +0.0697     0.9846      0.0876       --
       1.2615             +2.5733     3.0607      2.0399       --
       2.0000             +4.6000     3.5326      2.3000       --
  THE INVERSION FAILS, AND HERE IS WHY.  Over the range where the linear forced-orbit calculation is valid -- e_N up to a few tenths, where the forced streaming stays small compared with the circular speed -- |C_pred| never exceeds 0.38 km/s/kpc, which is 11 times below the 2 sigma limit |C| < 4.0.  So C places NO constraint at all on e_N below ~0.5.  Beyond that the response turns over and changes sign (see the table), because the external field stops being a perturbation -- so the formal crossing near e_N ~ 1.3 is outside the regime the calculation can be trusted in and must not be quoted as a bound.
  [PASS] 98d (AGAINST INTEREST -- the result this item was supposed to deliver does NOT exist) the Oort constants give NO useful upper limit on the Milky Way's external field.  Over the whole range where the linear response calculation is valid the predicted C stays two orders of magnitude below the measured value and its error, so the data are consistent with any external field the framework could plausibly have; and where |C_pred| finally becomes comparable to the measurement the perturbation is no longer small and the estimator is invalid.  The bound is not merely weak, it is absent   (max |C_pred| = 0.377 km/s/kpc over e_N < 0.6, against a 2 sigma allowance of 4.0; the reconstructed field e_N = 0.0127 gives 0.0138, 290 times too small to be seen)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
mutation controls
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  MUTATION 1 (external field switched off): C_pred falls from -0.0138 to -1.86e-06 km/s/kpc -- with no external field the potential is axisymmetric and C and K vanish identically, as they must
  MUTATION 2 (put the Sun at psi = 0, in line with the field instead of across it): C and K carry a factor sin(m psi) and would vanish; the whole effect would move into A and B, where the errors are the same size.  The estimator is therefore not a generic 'something is non-axisymmetric' detector -- it is tied to the field's direction on the sky
  MUTATION 3 (e_N = 0.5, a cluster-strength field): C_pred = -0.377 km/s/kpc, i.e. 27 times larger -- the estimator responds when the physics is made large, so the small answer above is a statement about the Local Group and not about the code
  MUTATION 4 (linearity): C_pred/e_N is constant to 0.9 per cent over e_N = 0.005-0.05, which is exactly what a linear response to the external field must give -- so the small answer is the physics and not a numerical collapse
  [PASS] 98e MUTATION CONTROLS behave: with no external field C and K vanish to machine precision; the predicted C is proportional to e_N in the linear regime, as a linear response must be; it grows by more than an order of magnitude at cluster field strength; and it is tied to the field's sky direction through sin(m psi) rather than being a generic non-axisymmetry detector   (zero-field C = -1.86e-06; C/e_N constant to 0.9% over e_N = 0.005-0.05; cluster-field C = -0.377 against -0.0138 km/s/kpc, a factor 27)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERDICT.  Item 98's premise is WITHDRAWN as posed -- the wide-binary EFE tensor at x_ext = 1.9 is not
what the Oort constants measure, and using it would over-state the effect a hundredfold.  Done properly:
the Milky Way's real external field, e_N = 0.0127, forces streaming motions of order 1 km/s at the
Sun and contributes |C| = 0.014 km/s/kpc, 0.4 per cent of the measured C = -3.2 +- 0.4, which is
conventionally spiral and bar structure.  UNDERPOWERED as a detection by a factor of two hundred.
AND THE INVERSE DOES NOT RESCUE IT.  The obvious salvage -- read the Oort constants as an upper limit on
the Galaxy's external field -- fails: over the whole range where the linear response is valid, |C_pred|
never exceeds 0.38 km/s/kpc against a 2 sigma allowance of 4.0, so the data permit any e_N the framework
could have.  Item 98 yields a corrected premise and a null, and no constraint in either direction.
Caveats stated rather than buried: the response is computed without the disc's
own self-gravity, which amplifies m = 1 distortions by a factor of a few; the epicyclic approximation is
used; a steady state is assumed; and the point-mass QUMOND solver stands in for a disc, matched to the
measured internal acceleration radius by radius.  Every one of those makes the bound softer, not harder.
